            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.329,
            0.848,
            0.421
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have seen how algebra makes use of letter symbols to write general statements about patterns and relations in a compact manner. Algebra can also be used to justify or prove claims and conjectures (like the many properties you saw in the previous chapter) and to solve problems of various kinds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.421,
            0.849,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Distributivity is a property relating multiplication and addition that is captured concisely using algebra. In this chapter, we explore different types of multiplication patterns and show how they can be described in the language of algebra by making use of distributivity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.511,
            0.665,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.1 Some Properties of Multiplication"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.548,
            0.44,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Increments in Products"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.578,
            0.702,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider the multiplication of two numbers, say, 23 × 27."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.609,
            0.847,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. By how much does the product increase if the first number (23) is increased by 1?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.661,
            0.148,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.111,
                0.661,
                0.148,
                0.688
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_0_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.664,
            0.662,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. What if the second number (27) is increased by 1?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.698,
            0.148,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.111,
                0.698,
                0.148,
                0.723
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_0_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.7,
            0.68,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. How about when both numbers are increased by 1?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.736,
            0.846,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Do you see a pattern that could help generalise our observations to the product of any two numbers?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.779,
            0.848,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us first consider a simpler problem—find the increase in the product when 27 is increased by 1. From the definition of multiplication (and the commutative property), it is clear that the product increases by 23. This can be seen from the distributive property of multiplication as well. If a, b and c are three numbers, then —"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.971,
            0.551,
